{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Assemble the people, the men and the women and the little ones.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥagigah 3a): the men came to learn, the women to hear; the little ones — why did they come? To give reward to those who bring them. This implies that it is speaking of little ones who have not yet reached the age of education, for otherwise there would have been no need to give the reason 'to give reward' etc. And this is a great difficulty, for it is as though He commanded that they be loaded with a burden of wood and stones for the house of YHWH, merely in order to give reward to those who bring them." "And it appears that the primary purpose of this assembly was for the sake of repentance, for our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 30) that the first day of the Festival is called 'first for the reckoning of transgressions'; therefore they need to make immediate preparation for repentance, and this preparation is this assembly, in which the king gathers the people and reads before them from 'אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים,' for most of the book consists of words of reproof concerning sin and words of humbling. And it is already well known that the assembly has great relevance to repentance, for through their being assembled they become one union and incline together as one to return to YHWH, so that those who repent are many — because specifically during the ten days between Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts the repentance of the individual, but during all the days of the year the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts only the repentance of the many (Rosh HaShanah 18a). And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'וּלְקַחְתֶּם לָכֶם בַּיּוֹם הָרִאשׁוֹן' — is it then first? Is it not the fifteenth? Rather, first for the reckoning of transgressions etc. And why did Scripture mention this matter specifically in the verse speaking of the taking of the four species, and why did it not mention this in the verse speaking of dwelling in the sukkah? Rather, it is because in the taking of the four species all four classes of Israel are alluded to, who will be in one union, as explained above in parashat Emor (Leviticus 23:40); and the verse needed to give good reason and understanding why specifically on this day they were commanded to perform this commandment, which contains the indication that all Israel should be one union so that these would atone for those — only because from Rosh HaShanah to Yom HaKippurim the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts even the repentance of the individual, therefore they do not yet need to be one union so that those who repent be many; and from Yom HaKippurim to the Festival of Sukkot they do not commit transgressions, for each person is occupied with the commandment of sukkah and lulav, and one commandment draws another commandment, therefore presumably no sin will befall the righteous until the first day of Sukkot, which is 'first for the reckoning of transgressions.' Therefore they need to make preparation for repentance, and this preparation is that they all be one union like these four species. Therefore it says: 'וּלְקַחְתֶּם לָכֶם בַּיּוֹם הָרִאשׁוֹן' — as though giving a reason for praise: why have I commanded you regarding the taking of these four species bound together specifically on this day? Because it is the first day for the reckoning of transgressions, therefore you need to make preparation for the repentance of all the days of the year, and this preparation is when you are in one union like these four species — then YHWH will accept your repentance, for He is a mighty El who does not despise." "And this is also the reason for this assembly, for in addition to the taking of these four species on the first day of the Festival, YHWH commanded to make another impression similar to this at the end of seven years, for the Sabbatical year also brings about assembly and peace — through that one neither sows nor reaps in it, and 'the poor of your people shall eat' — for one is not permitted to retain the produce of the seventh year as a property owner; and this is without doubt the cause of peace, for all matters of strife are drawn from the trait of 'mine is mine' — this one says 'all of it is mine.' But in the seventh year all this is not so, for in matters of rising and doing, all are not equal, but in matters of sitting and not doing, all are equal. And this is in truth the essence of peace; and likewise at the Festival of Sukkot, when each person goes out from a permanent dwelling to a temporary dwelling and sits under his sukkah of peace — behold, on the first day of the intermediate days of the Festival, the king was commanded to make an impression toward peace. And this is the meaning of the assembly, for all this is preparation for repentance, and he reads before them from 'אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים' in words of humbling and reproof. And this is what they said: the men came to learn etc., the little ones — why did they come? To give reward to those who bring them — for when Israel do repentance, they then entreat mercy before Him, blessed be He, for forgiveness of sin, and they say: If not for our sake He should do it, then let Him do it for the sake of the sucklings who have not sinned, as is found in the text of 'Avinu Malkeinu, have compassion on us and on our infants and our little ones,' and likewise they say: 'עֲשֵׂה לְמַעַן גְּמוּלֵי חָלָב' etc. And this is the reward given to those who bring them from the little ones — that they say before Him, blessed be He: Do it for the sake of these little ones who are brought to the house of YHWH, in the manner stated in Joel (Joel 2:16): 'Gather the people, sanctify the congregation, assemble the elders, gather the children and those who suck at the breast' etc." "And according to its plain sense, it speaks of little ones who have already reached the age of education, as it says: 'לְמַעַן יִשְׁמְעוּ' — these are the women; 'וּלְמַעַן יִלְמְדוּ' — these are the men; 'וּבְנֵיהֶם אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָדְעוּ יִשְׁמְעוּ וְלָמְדוּ' — these are the little ones who have reached the age of education."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:33.824206746Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}